<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talakad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Talakadu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Talakad is a town on the Kaveri river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, once the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> took it around 1004, and its capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a> in 1116 drove them from southern Karnataka; he took the title Talakadugonda, conqueror of Talakad. Many of its old temples now lie buried under drifting sand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talakad.jpg" alt="Talakad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaidyeshvara_Temple_(1000_AD)_at_Talakad.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Talakadu lies on the left bank of the Kaveri, 45 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and 133 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, at a point where the eastward-flowing river changes course and the sand on its banks spreads over a wide area. The town once had more than 30 temples. The old city lies buried beneath sand for nearly a mile, with only the tops of two gopuras visible; the sand hills used to advance 9 or 10 feet a year, mainly during the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, forcing people to abandon their houses and move inland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One tradition derives the name from two Kirāta twin brothers, Tala and Kādu, who cut down a tree that wild elephants worshipped and found in it an image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> form of the name is Dalavana-pura. Its earliest authentic mention is with the Ganga kings. According to an old chronicle, Harivarma, assigned to 247–266 CE, was installed at Skandapura but lived at Dalavanapura, and later kings of the line were crowned there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> renamed the captured town Rājarājapura. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a> took it, Talakadu was made up of seven towns and five mathas, and it stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> until the mid-fourteenth century before passing to a feudatory of the Vijayanagar rulers.</p><p>In 1610 Rāja Wodeyar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> took Talakadu. According to the story told of it, Rāni Alamelamma, wife of the Vijayanagar representative Tirumala-Rāja, refused him her jewels, threw them into the Kaveri and drowned herself opposite Mālangi, cursing that Talakadu should become sand, Mālangi a whirlpool and the Mysore kings be without heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The surviving group of temples is a popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage site, and the town has grown with the wet cultivation watered by the Mādhavamantri anicut. The Kírti Nārāyana temple has been excavated, the Vydyanatheshwara temple is the most imposing left uncovered, and the Ānandēsvara and Gaurisankara temples were unearthed in the early nineteenth century; an inscription dates the Gaurisankara to the reign of Chikka-Dēva-Rāja-Wodeyar (1672–1704).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Talakadu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first chiefs known from inscriptions, Nripa Kama II in the early eleventh century and his son Vinayaditya, who ruled c. 1047–1098, were feudatories of the Western Chalukya Empire and fought in its wars against the Chola Empire, which then held the old Ganga country around <b>Talakad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The defining conflict of Pallava history was a long struggle with the Chalukyas of Badami for supremacy in the south, fought alongside wars with the Pandyas of Madurai, the Gangas of <b>Talakad</b> and, later, the Rashtrakutas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their early capital was Kolar, later moved to <b>Talakad</b> on the Kaveri.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
